# systemd-networkd runs for the USB network gadget's usb0 only (usr/lib/systemd/network/
# 10-qforge-usb-gadget.network); no other interface has a .network file, so it touches nothing
# NetworkManager owns. qforge-usb-gadget.service itself is static: udev starts it when a UDC appears.
#
# The Holo base ships systemd-networkd.service MASKED (etc/systemd/system/systemd-networkd.service ->
# /dev/null; SteamOS runs NetworkManager). A preset cannot enable a masked unit ("Unit ... is masked,
# ignoring" on the Air X's first boot, 2026-10-10), so the overlay replaces that symlink with one to
# the real unit (etc/systemd/system/systemd-networkd.service -> /usr/lib/systemd/system/...).
# systemd-networkd-wait-online stays masked: nothing should wait for usb0.
#
# No `enable` line here: a unit reached through that symlink is a LINKED unit to systemd, and
# preset-all refuses to enable those ("Refusing to operate on linked unit file", once per first
# boot on the Air X). The multi-user.target.wants symlink in etc/systemd/system is what enables
# it, and that works on a linked unit. This file exists to document the arrangement.
